<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fuad I of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1936">1936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ahmad Fuad I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fuad I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, of the Muhammad Ali dynasty, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Egypt under the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1917 and took the title King of Egypt after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s declaration of independence in 1922. He clashed repeatedly with the nationalist Wafd party over the 1923 constitution, which he suspended for a time. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> University's royal predecessor institutions and was succeeded by his son Farouk.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fuad-i-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Fuad I of Egypt" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brain News Service (publisher) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fuad_I_of_Egypt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
